     1	# Deferred-work bundle: brief-derivation-concurrency
     2	
     3	bundle_name: brief-derivation-concurrency
     4	dw_ids: DW-112
     5	
     6	## Intent
     7	
     8	Make first-time Brief persistence idempotent by project and inputs hash. convex/generations.ts:1565 checks reuse in a separate query, while :1684 inserts version 1 unconditionally. Recheck inside the persistence transaction and stamp concurrent generations to the authoritative Brief, with a concurrent-first-derivation regression.
     9	
    10	## Ledger entries (verbatim)
    11	
    12	### DW-112: Two generations that concurrently derive the same brand-new (projectId, inputsHash) for the first time can each insert a version-1 Brief.
    13	origin: spec-deferred 46a3dcf375ac
    14	location: convex/generations.ts (findReusableBrief, persistDerivedBrief)
    15	source_spec: `1-generation-brief-storage-and-derivation-stage.md`
    16	severity: medium
    17	reason: persistDerivedBrief unconditionally inserts a new generationBriefs row without re-checking for an existing row inside its own transaction; the reuse check (findReusableBrief) runs earlier, in a separate action call. Two concurrent first-time derivations for the same key could each pass that check before either persists, leaving MAX(version) reuse and saveEntryEdit's staleness check ambiguous between the two rows.
    18	status: open
    20	
    21	**Problem:** Two generations can both miss `findReusableBrief` for the same brand-new `(projectId, inputsHash)` and each attempt to publish a version-1 Brief. Duplicate first versions make keyed reuse and writer-edit staleness ambiguous.
    22	
    23	**Approach:** Reuse one keyed Brief lookup inside `persistDerivedBrief` before its project-wide baseline fence. The first committed row becomes authoritative; a concurrent publisher writes no Brief or entry rows, stamps its generation to the authoritative Brief, and returns that id.
    24	
    25	## Boundaries & Constraints
    26	
    27	**Always:** The idempotency decision occurs in the same Convex mutation transaction as publication and uses `by_projectId_and_inputsHash`; the authoritative row is the latest stored version for that key; both winning and losing generations end with the authoritative `briefId`; existing changed-input baseline fencing remains intact.
    28	
    29	**Block If:** The real mutation boundary cannot be exercised under concurrent first-publication scheduling in `convex-test`.
    30	
    31	**Never:** Add a schema uniqueness surrogate, call a separate query from the mutation, rerun the model, create version 2 for a duplicate derivation, change writer-edit/version semantics, modify generated files, mutate report prose, or edit the deferred-work ledger.
    32	
    33	## I/O & Edge-Case Matrix
    34	
    35	| Scenario | Input / State | Expected Output / Behavior | Error Handling |
    36	|----------|---------------|----------------------------|----------------|
    37	| Concurrent first publication | Two generations for one project and hash have both observed no reusable Brief | Exactly one version-1 Brief is stored; both calls return it and both generations reference it | The losing publisher writes no Brief entries and adopts the committed row |
    38	| Existing same-key version | Persistence begins after any Brief version already exists for the key | Return and stamp the latest same-key version without publishing candidates | No project-baseline retry is needed |
    39	| No same-key version, stale project baseline | Another Brief with different inputs became newest after baseline pinning | Preserve the current `null` return so `publishDerivedBrief` rereads and retries | No rows or generation stamp are written |
    40	
    41	</intent-contract>
    42	
    43	## Code Map
    44	
    45	- `convex/generations.ts:1663-1677` (`findReusableBrief`): existing descending lookup on `by_projectId_and_inputsHash`; extract or share its mechanics with persistence.
    46	- `convex/generations.ts:1785-1797` (`newestProjectBrief`): project-wide changed-input fence that must remain after the same-key idempotency check.
    47	- `convex/generations.ts:1910-2071` (`persistDerivedBrief`): transaction boundary and only derived Brief publisher; return/stamp the authoritative same-key row before validation or inserts.
    48	- `convex/ai/brief.ts:379-406` (`publishDerivedBrief`): caller retries only on a lost project-baseline fence; an authoritative id must be treated as success.
    49	- `convex/ai/brief.test.ts:916-982`: existing real `convexTest` publish adapter and fixtures to reuse for the concurrent regression.
    50	- `convex/schema.ts:2221-2267`: existing non-unique keyed index is sufficient because Convex mutations serialize conflicting reads and writes; read-only.
    51	- `_bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/deferred-work.md`: native orchestrator-owned ledger; read-only and byte-preserved.
    52	
    53	## Tasks & Acceptance
    54	
    55	**Execution:**
    56	- [x] `convex/generations.ts`: share the latest same-key lookup between `findReusableBrief` and `persistDerivedBrief`; in persistence, stamp and return an existing row before the baseline fence and all candidate processing.
    57	- [x] `convex/ai/brief.test.ts`: add a synchronized concurrent-first-derivation regression through the real `persistDerivedBrief` mutation boundary, without assuming which caller wins.
    58	
    59	**Acceptance Criteria:**
    60	- Given two generations for one project and identical inputs have both observed no reusable Brief, when their first publications race, then exactly one version-1 Brief exists for the key and both generations reference its id.
    61	- Given a same-key Brief already exists when persistence starts, when candidates and a stale baseline are supplied, then persistence returns and stamps the latest same-key Brief without inserting parent or child rows.
    62	- Given only a different-key Brief invalidates the pinned project baseline, when persistence runs, then it returns `null`, writes nothing, and preserves the caller's retry contract.
    63	
    64	## Spec Change Log
    65	

